From 7fba8067f4d7d0f78c5bd43ce58be35509d73afa Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Stefan Hertenberger <erde74@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 08:34:24 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 25/27] ip: closeconv keeps its reference until the
 conversation is Idle

tcpclose can drop the conversation's lock (tcpoutput does, around
ipoput, to send the FIN). closeconv had already taken inuse to 0, so
when the ack of the FIN came in meanwhile (Last_ack -> Closed in the
loopback reader) the conversation looked free and Fsprotoclone gave it
to another process dialing. closeconv then went on and set it Idle,
undoing that dialer's Connecting/Connected: connectctlmsg slept for
ever on "connected", with the TCP side Established (often spliced).

Seen as dial(2) over loopback hanging under load: four procs
listening, accepting and closing, twelve procs each dialing 200 times
hung after about a thousand dials in every run; with this all pass.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 sys/src/9/ip/devip.c | 12 +++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/sys/src/9/ip/devip.c b/sys/src/9/ip/devip.c
index ac7fe9320..165982520 100644
--- a/sys/src/9/ip/devip.c
+++ b/sys/src/9/ip/devip.c
@@ -595,7 +595,16 @@ closeconv(Conv *cv)
 
 	qlock(cv);
 
-	if(--cv->inuse > 0) {
+	/*
+	 * keep our reference until the conversation is Idle:
+	 * the protocol's close can let go of the lock (tcpoutput does, to send
+	 * the FIN), and with inuse 0 and the protocol done (an ack of the FIN
+	 * meanwhile) Fsprotoclone would give it out again; setting it Idle then
+	 * would undo a connect in progress on the new owner's side, which then
+	 * sleeps for ever in connectctlmsg.
+	 */
+	if(cv->inuse > 1) {
+		cv->inuse--;
 		qunlock(cv);
 		return;
 	}
@@ -617,6 +626,7 @@ closeconv(Conv *cv)
 		(*cv->p->close)(cv);
 
 	cv->state = Idle;
+	cv->inuse--;
 	qunlock(cv);
 }
 
